            <ArticleTitle>Case of spontaneous healing of occlusive dissection on percutaneous transluminal coronary angioplasty.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>249-54</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A large pseudolumen due to dissection arose during percutaneous coronary angioplasty. The true lumen was compressed critically. However, no infarction took place because of good collaterals. During medical follow-up, the patient's angina vanished suddenly. The repeat angiography showed healing of the dissection and patency of the coronary artery.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
